//? Title: Modular exponentiation
//? Subtitle: Actual binary powering, exact residues and counted products
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Computing an integer power modulo a positive integer should not require
constructing the enormous unreduced power. We derive binary powering, inspect
its actual bit transitions, retain the original power examples, and compare
measured operation counts with the exact bit-count formula. The implementation
covers every unsigned 64-bit input using complete 128-bit intermediate products.
)

\toc

> The invariant behind binary powering

Write e as a sum of powers of two. At every loop entry, let z be the
accumulator, b the running base and q the unprocessed exponent. The invariant is

$$
z b^q \equiv a^e \pmod m.
$$
\alttext(The accumulator times the running base raised to the remaining exponent always equals the requested power modulo the modulus.)

Initially z=1, b=a modulo m, q=e. For q=2t, keep z and replace b by b squared
and q by t. For q=2t+1, first multiply z by b, then make the same replacements.
These preserve the invariant. The nonnegative exponent halves until zero, when z
is the requested residue. This proof does not require a prime modulus or a
coprime base.

> Three bits that can be checked by hand

For 5 to the sixth power modulo23, the low-to-high bits are0,1,1. The running
bases are5,2,4; the accumulator is1,2,8 after the three folds. The native trace
below records the states of the same kernel used by the ordinary function. It
also records the final square to16, although no subsequent iteration uses that
value.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/modpow_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::mod_pow_trace(5, 6, 23);
    std::print("| bit | remaining | base before | accumulator before | multiply | accumulator after | " "squared base |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (auto &s : t.steps) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.bit_index,
                   s.remaining_before, s.running_before, s.accumulator_before,
                   s.multiply, s.accumulator_after, s.squared_base);
        auto id = std::to_string(s.bit_index);
        d.node(id, {6.0 * s.bit_index, 0},
               std::format("Actual bit {}: {}\nbase {} / accumulator {}",
                           s.bit_index, s.multiply ? 1 : 0, s.running_before,
                           s.accumulator_after));
        if (s.bit_index)
            d.edge(std::to_string(s.bit_index - 1), id);
    }
    d.save_svg("build/modpow_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| bit | remaining | base before | accumulator before | multiply | accumulator after | squared base |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 6 | 5 | 1 | false | 1 | 2 |
| 1 | 3 | 2 | 1 | true | 2 | 4 |
| 2 | 1 | 4 | 2 | true | 8 | 16 |
\image(figures/modpow_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual bit transitions for5 to the sixth power modulo23.)
\alttext(Three actual states show a skipped multiply followed by two accumulator products.)

> The original four-bit example

The original7 to the thirteenth power modulo1000 returns407. Its three set bits
multiply the accumulator by7,401 and801. Four squares are performed, including
the unused final square. The table and figure read the actual trace rather than
implementing a second powering loop. The remaining spot checks include exponent
zero and a composite modulus.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/modpow_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::mod_pow_trace(7, 13, 1000);
    std::vector<double> bit, base, acc;
    std::print("| bit | multiply | base before | accumulator after | squared base |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &s : t.steps) {
        bit.push_back(s.bit_index);
        base.push_back(s.running_before);
        acc.push_back(s.accumulator_after);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.bit_index, s.multiply,
                   s.running_before, s.accumulator_after, s.squared_base);
    }
    std::print("\nResult: {}. Spot checks: {}, {}, {}.\n", t.result,
               dm::algorithms::mod_pow(2, 10, 1000),
               dm::algorithms::mod_pow(3, 0, 7),
               dm::algorithms::mod_pow(7, 256, 13));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(bit, base, "running base", {37, 99, 235}, 6).points(
        bit, acc, "accumulator after fold", {220, 38, 38}, 6).title(
        "Original 7 to power 13 modulo 1000: actual states").x_label(
        "bit index, low first").y_label("residue").save_svg(
        "build/modpow_original.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| bit | multiply | base before | accumulator after | squared base |
| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | true | 7 | 7 | 49 |
| 1 | false | 49 | 7 | 401 |
| 2 | true | 401 | 807 | 801 |
| 3 | true | 801 | 407 | 601 |

Result: 407. Spot checks: 24, 1, 9.
\image(figures/modpow_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original power uses four squares and three accumulator products.)
\alttext(Blue running bases and red accumulators come from the actual four-bit execution.)

> A finite multiplicative cycle

The original3 to power k modulo17 example visits every nonzero residue for
k=0..15 and returns to1 at k=16. Distinctness of those sixteen displayed
residues proves that3 has order16 in this particular group. This tiny cycle is
directly enumerable; its visual appearance provides no evidence for the
difficulty of discrete logarithms at cryptographic sizes.

Fermat's little theorem predicts a to the twelfth power modulo13 equals1 for
each base a=2..6. The native calls retain those original checks. Passing such
checks alone is not a primality certificate for an arbitrary modulus.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/modpow_cycle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| exponent | 3 to exponent modulo17 |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::uint64_t k = 0; k <= 16; ++k) {
        auto v = dm::algorithms::mod_pow(3, k, 17);
        x.push_back(k);
        y.push_back(v);
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", k, v);
    }
    std::print("\n| base | base to power12 modulo13 |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::uint64_t a = 2; a <= 6; ++a)
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", a, dm::algorithms::mod_pow(a, 12, 13));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual residue", {37, 99, 235}, 6).title(
        "Original finite cycle: powers of 3 modulo 17").x_label("exponent k").y_label("residue").save_svg("build/modpow_cycle.svg");
}
```
